          "type": "turn",
          "question": "A claim came back asking for more specificity on a three-character code. Is that always a reasonable request?",
          "answer": "No, it isn't always reasonable. Whether a request for more detail is valid depends on the code involved.\n\n**Some three-character codes are complete.** The coding guide says I10, essential hypertension, \"takes none\" of a fourth, fifth or sixth character. So a request to add more specificity to I10 \"is a request the classification cannot satisfy.\" (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*.)\n\n**Many three-character codes are only categories.** The same guide says most categories need a fourth, fifth or sixth character before the code is complete. Characters four and five add detail such as cause, body site or severity. Character six often records laterality, meaning right, left or unspecified. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.) A claim that says a category is incomplete may be correct.\n\n**Some categories do need more detail for a specific reason.** For example, the COPD guide says the fourth character of J44 records whether a complication is present at that visit. It also says the unspecified code is used for a stable presentation. (Source: *Coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: J44 and its two complication codes*.) The type 2 diabetes guide says that a diagnosis written only as \"diabetes mellitus,\" with no type given, defaults to type 2. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*.)\n\n**What I could not check:** These passages do not say which code your claim used, what the payer's rules are, or whether the reviewer read the record correctly. The documents are educational summaries and do not settle billing disputes. If you think the request is wrong, check the claim's code against the full official ICD-10-CM tables and the payer's instructions. A coder or billing specialist can also review the claim and the clinical notes.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
